# Kernel: sim
# Killer: none
#
# TASK-150.9: a DAX file's change of size has Fxfs zero its last block past the end by moving it
# (FXFS_SET_KEEP_TAIL dropped), from under the window, which goes on mapping it. Killed on writable
# DAX by fxfs-guest-checks-dax's append_stopped_short_in_a_page (GCE fxdax-test-t18-ac, 2026-11-03),
# where the window's page was the old block itself, written through. Equivalent copying on write
# (TASK-149.30): the flag changes only a cut to within a block (a growth zeroes the old end's block
# either way, and drops its range first, fxfs_dax_grow_begin), and fxfs_dax_resize_begin zeroes the
# last page past the new end through the window before Fxfs cuts (fxfs_dax_zero_part), which makes
# the page private, a copy of the old block, dirty. Nothing then reads the old block through the
# window: a private page goes only by a MAP or UNMAP, after its writeback, and the window maps then
# what Fxfs holds, never the moved block's old place. If writeback and a rebind come between the
# zeroing and the cut, the page maps a block that holds zeros past the end already, which Fxfs's
# txn_zero_tail leaves where it is (and the model's move_tail). So the move costs a block written,
# and a crash between keeps the file's bytes either way. As it was: passes alone in 3 rounds of
# fxfs-guest-checks-dax (GCE fxdax-test-r625, 2026-11-05), and every guest/sim one-run test and
# scenario, and 300 seeds of interleave and failures.

--- a/guest/linux/fs/fxfs/data.c
+++ b/guest/linux/fs/fxfs/data.c
@@ -40,7 +40,7 @@
 	truncate_setsize(inode, size);
 	if (iattr)
 		setattr_copy(idmap, inode, iattr);
-	err = fxfs_vfs_write_attr(inode, FXFS_SET_SIZE | (ops->keeps_tail ? FXFS_SET_KEEP_TAIL : 0));
+	err = fxfs_vfs_write_attr(inode, FXFS_SET_SIZE);
 	/*
 	 * fxdax: with no room, a file grown goes back to its size: nothing is past its old end yet,
 	 * and the inode saying a size Fxfs hasn't would lose it at eviction. A cut borrows the room
